. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H1 * 
. * This section tests whether having daughters leads to stronger support for g
> ender equality (H1). 
.                 * 
.                 * Dependent Variable:  
.                 * There is no SOEP survey item that directly measures support
>  for gender equality. 
.                 * As a proxy, we use the item plh0174, where respondents repo
> rt their satisfaction 
.                 * with the current division of housework.  
.                 * We construct the binary variable satis_housew, coded as 1 i
> f the respondent reports 
.                 * high satisfaction (values 8–10), and 0 otherwise. A value o
> f 1 is interpreted as 
.                 * an attitudinal indicator of perceived fairness in the domes
> tic arrangement, 
.                 * and thus as a proxy for a supportive stance toward gender-b
> alanced domestic roles.
.                 * This assumes that satisfaction reflects personal approval o
> f the arrangement, 
.                 * not necessarily objective equality.
.                 * 
.                 * Model Specification:  
.                 * We estimate a logistic regression to test whether having da
> ughters is associated 
.                 * with greater support for gender equality. To isolate the ef
> fect of daughters from 
.                 * family size effects, we include total number of children fi
> xed-effects (i.tot_kids). 
.                 * Standard errors are clustered at the respondent level to ac
> count for repeated 
.                 * observations within individuals in the panel. The model is 
> specified as follows:  
. 
. logit satis_housew daughters_nr `controls' i.tot_kids, cluster(pid)

note: 12.tot_kids != 0 predicts success perfectly;
      12.tot_kids omitted and 13 obs not used.

